---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Interactive gears in a browser are two discs with teeth that mesh so spinning one turns the
      other. This page opens a 12-tooth driver against a 24-tooth driven gear. The live ratio reads
      1 : 2. Drag either gear. The pair stays meshed because tooth count owns radius.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example: Input: a 12 and 24 tooth pair on a phone-width tab. Output: a reverse-direction
      spin at half speed, optional click sound, and a Reset layout that restores the default pair
      without a reload.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore this is a fidget and a light teaching toy, not a CAD worksheet. It is free, needs
      no download, and uploads nothing about how you spun it.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Gears →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-is">
    <h2>What are interactive gears? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A physical gear train is teeth on a pitch circle. The number of teeth and the radius have to
      agree or the teeth will not meet. Interactive gears online should follow the same rule. Many
      applets do not: they let you drag size independently, so two discs overlap and the ratio
      label keeps lying.
    </p>
    <p>
      This pair is built from tooth count. Twelve teeth set one radius. Twenty-four teeth set
      twice that radius. The centres sit one sum of radii apart. Change the counts with the driver
      and driven buttons. A pair that would not fit the stage is refused, and the previous layout
      stays.
    </p>
    <p>
      People search for a gear ratio simulator when they want to see 1 : 2, not compute it. A
      meshing gears page that hides the numbers is a toy that forgot the one fact the user came
      for. The line under the stage is that fact, in tabular numerals.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How do meshing gears work here? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Drag on the stage. Pointer movement around the driver centre becomes angular velocity. The
      driven gear uses the negative tooth ratio, so an external mesh reverses. Friction from the
      Feel engine lets the pair coast and then stop, the same rest idea as the fidget spinner.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sound is optional. A click can fire when you change teeth. Mute under the board or Settings
      → Feel. Haptics stay separate, off by default, because most laptops have no vibrator.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reduced motion (system or Settings → Feel) turns continuous spin off. A Step control appears
      so you can advance one tooth. The mesh and the ratio stay. Interactions still work; the
      decoration stops.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Tooth count owns radius">
      <p>
        If radius and teeth can disagree, two gears overlap without meshing. The guardrail is that
        this page never lets them disagree. A refused pair is a toast, not a broken picture.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="ratio">
    <h2>How do I read the gear ratio? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The ratio is driver teeth to driven teeth, reduced. 12 and 24 is 1 : 2. 8 and 12 is 2 : 3.
      It is a count ratio, not a speedometer. The driven disc turns slower when it has more teeth,
      and the other way because the mesh is external.
    </p>
    <p>
      Engineering applets often bury that line in a table. Toys often omit it. This fidget keeps
      it on the status row so a parent can point at it without opening a textbook.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Reading the ratio as a speed">
      <p>
        1 : 2 names teeth, not kilometres per hour. The driven gear turns at half the driver angle
        for a 12 and 24 pair. The sign is negative. If the label did not update after a teeth
        change, the toy would be lying.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="play">
    <h2>What does Play do on gears? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Play hides the trust row, knowledge drawers, footer, and Feel strip so the gears fill the
      space under the tool name. The ratio stays, because it is the answer. Mute stays in a
      corner. Exit or Escape restores the page. There is no native fullscreen requirement; the
      chrome-hide is the mode.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use it when the rest of the page is the distraction. A phone in one hand can flick the pair
      without scrolling past related tools. Safe-area padding keeps Exit above the home indicator.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>Try the pair <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Open the tool, drag the 12-tooth gear, watch the 24-tooth follow the other way, change a
      tooth count, and hit Reset layout if you want the default pair back. Mute if you need the
      room quiet. That is the whole product.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Gears →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
